  412-drill-10) unchanged. Learning outcomes unchanged.
assessments:
  - discriminant: mcq
    value:
      id: 412-mcq-01
      prompt: "An employee at a retail company guesses their line manager's password and logs into the manager's email account to read messages about an upcoming promotion decision. The employee then logs out without sending or altering anything. Which Act has the employee primarily breached?"
      options:
        - The Computer Misuse Act 1990
        - The Data Protection Act 2018 and UK GDPR
        - The Equality Act 2010
        - The Copyright, Designs and Patents Act 1988
      correct_index: 0
      feedback_correct: |
        Correct. The employee has committed an offence under section 1 of
        the Computer Misuse Act 1990: unauthorised access to computer
        material. The offence is complete at the point of access; reading
        without copying or altering is enough. The fact that the employee
        works for the same company is irrelevant because authorisation is
        task-specific, and the employee was not authorised to access the
        manager's account.
      feedback_incorrect: |
        The answer is the Computer Misuse Act 1990. Many candidates pick
        the Data Protection Act because the manager's email contains
        personal data. The DPA is engaged for the company (as data
        controller it may have failed to enforce access controls) but the
        primary statutory offence committed by the employee is the CMA
        section 1 offence of unauthorised access to computer material.
        The Equality Act and the Copyright, Designs and Patents Act are
        not engaged at all.
      difficulty: standard
  - discriminant: nei
    value:
      id: 412-nei-01
      stimulus: |
        An IT support worker at a secondary school is authorised to access
        teacher email accounts in order to reset forgotten passwords as
        part of their job. One evening, after a workplace dispute with
        the headteacher, the same worker logs in from home to the
        headteacher's email account and reads through several weeks of
        private messages. Nothing is copied, altered or shared.
      question: |
        Name the Act and the specific section the IT worker has breached,
        explain why the worker's existing access privileges do not protect
        them, and describe the impact for the worker and the school.
      max_marks: 4
      mark_scheme:
        name_expected: |
          The Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 1 (unauthorised access to
          computer material). Credit answers that name the Act and
          identify the basic offence even if the section number is not
          given.
        explain_expected: |
          Authorisation under the CMA is task-specific, not
          person-specific. The IT worker was authorised to access teacher
          mailboxes for the specific purpose of password resets as part of
          their support role. Using those same technical credentials to
          read the headteacher's private correspondence for a personal
          reason is outside the scope of the authorisation, and the
          worker knew it was outside that scope, so the access is
          unauthorised. The section 1 offence is complete at the point of
          access; no copying, altering or sharing is required to commit
          the offence.
      impact_expected: |
          For the worker, the impact is criminal liability under section 1
          (summary or indictable conviction, up to two years' imprisonment,
          a fine, or both), almost certain dismissal for gross misconduct,
          permanent professional reference damage, and likely bar from
          future safeguarding-relevant roles. For the school, the impact
          is a likely Data Protection Act 2018 exposure as data controller
          (insufficient access controls allowed misuse of legitimate
          credentials), loss of trust within the staff body, a need to
          rebuild the IT access policy with stricter logging and auditing,
          the cost of a forensic review of what the worker accessed
          during the unauthorised session, and possible ICO scrutiny if
          the incident is reported.
      exemplar_answer: |
        The IT worker has breached the Computer Misuse Act 1990, section
        1 (unauthorised access to computer material). Although the worker
        holds legitimate technical access to teacher email accounts for
        the purpose of password resets, authorisation under the CMA is
        task-specific. Using the same credentials to read the
        headteacher's private email out of personal interest, after a
        workplace dispute, is outside the scope of the authorisation, and
        the worker plainly knows it. That brings the act inside section 1,
        which requires only knowing unauthorised access to computer
        material to be complete. The fact that nothing was copied or
        shared is irrelevant. The impact for the worker is criminal
        liability of up to two years' imprisonment under section 1,
        dismissal for gross misconduct, lasting reputational damage, and
        likely exclusion from future roles requiring trust. The impact
        for the school is a parallel Data Protection Act exposure for
        weak access controls, loss of internal trust, an obligation to
        rebuild the IT access policy with proper auditing, and possible
        ICO action if the incident is reported.
drill:
  - id: 412-drill-01
    cue: The offence of accessing computer material without authorisation, knowing the access to be unauthorised.
    answer: Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 1 (the basic offence)
  - id: 412-drill-02
    cue: The offence of unauthorised access with the intent to commit or facilitate a further criminal offence.
    answer: Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 2
  - id: 412-drill-03
    cue: The offence of doing an unauthorised act with the intent to impair the operation of a computer, or recklessness as to whether it is impaired.
    answer: Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 3
  - id: 412-drill-04
    cue: The offence of doing an unauthorised act that causes or creates a significant risk of serious damage of a material kind (added by the Serious Crime Act 2015).
    answer: Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 3ZA
  - id: 412-drill-05
    cue: The offence of making, supplying or obtaining articles (such as malware or hacking tools) for use in a CMA section 1, 2 or 3 offence.
    answer: Computer Misuse Act 1990, section 3A
  - id: 412-drill-06
    cue: The principle that authorisation under the Computer Misuse Act is tied to the purpose of access, not to whether a person holds login credentials.
    answer: Task-specific authorisation (exceeding authorisation is unauthorised access)
  - id: 412-drill-07
    cue: The mental element required for a section 1 offence under the Computer Misuse Act.
    answer: Knowledge that the access is unauthorised (mens rea)
  - id: 412-drill-08
    cue: The reason the Computer Misuse Act 1990 does not define the word "computer".
    answer: The Act was drafted deliberately broadly so it applies to any computing device (including phones, smartcards and IoT devices), regardless of technology generation
  - id: 412-drill-09
    cue: The maximum sentence available under section 3ZA of the Computer Misuse Act 1990, in cases involving damage to human welfare or national security.
    answer: Life imprisonment
  - id: 412-drill-10
    cue: The 1988 House of Lords decision that exposed the gap in English law and led directly to the Computer Misuse Act 1990.
    answer: R v Gold and Schifreen (1988), which held that hacking by deception was not covered by the Forgery and Counterfeiting Act 1981
---

Six panels and a closer. The Computer Misuse Act 1990 lives next door
to the Data Protection Act. Most Paper 1 slips happen at that boundary.

{% panels %}

{% panel num="01_BOUNDARY" eyebrow="WHEN THE CMA APPLIES" headline="When [individuals] reach into systems they should not." illo="cma-boundary" %}
- DPA :: organisation handles personal data badly
- CMA :: individual accesses a system without authority
- Both :: name both, lead with the act being described

The boundary is not who is hurt. It is what is being done. Data sitting in a leaky database is a DPA problem for the controller. A person who deliberately reaches into someone else's mailbox is a CMA problem for that person.

{% takeaway %}
If the scenario shows a person reaching into a system they have no right to be in, the CMA is the lead Act. Name the section.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="02_FIVE" eyebrow="FIVE OFFENCES, ONE ACT" headline="[Five sections] of the CMA 1990." illo="cma-five" %}
{% term-card %}
s1 unauthorised access :: 2y
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s2 access with intent to commit a further offence :: 5y
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s3 unauthorised act with intent to impair (malware, DDoS, ransomware) :: 10y
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s3ZA unauthorised act causing serious damage to welfare, environment, economy or national security :: life (added 2015)
{% /term-card %}

{% term-card %}
s3A making, supplying or obtaining hacking articles :: 2y (added 2006)
{% /term-card %}

{% takeaway %}
The maximum sentence rises with the seriousness of what the person intended or caused. The section number is the Name mark.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="03_UNAUTH" eyebrow="WHAT UNAUTHORISED MEANS" headline="Authorisation is to a [task], not to a person." illo="cma-unauth" %}
{% def %}
**Unauthorised** access means the person is not entitled to control access of the kind in question and does not have consent from someone who is. Authorisation under the Act attaches to the task, not to the credentials.
{% /def %}

A bank clerk authorised to view customer accounts to process enquiries is not authorised to view a celebrity's account out of curiosity. A police officer authorised to query the PNC for operational reasons is not authorised to query a neighbour's record. Both have lawful logins. Both commit the section 1 offence the moment they go outside the task scope and know they are outside it.

{% takeaway %}
Exceeding the scope of legitimate access is unauthorised access. The credentials do not save the insider. This is the part Paper 1 tests most often.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="04_COMPUTER" eyebrow="WHAT A COMPUTER IS" headline="[Any device] that processes digital information." illo="cma-computer" %}
The Act does not define the word "computer". This is deliberate. The 1990 drafters left the term open so the legislation would not date as technology changed. Courts have since applied the Act to smartphones, smartcards, payment terminals, networked appliances and embedded systems alike.

{% term-card %}
phone :: smartcard :: payment terminal :: IoT device :: laptop
{% /term-card %}

{% takeaway %}
If the scenario describes any digital device, treat it as a computer for CMA purposes. Do not lose marks arguing about the device type.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="05_MENS" eyebrow="THE MENTAL ELEMENT" headline="The person must [know] the access is unauthorised." illo="cma-mens" %}
{% def %}
**Mens rea** under section 1 requires that the person knows the access is unauthorised at the point of access. Accidental access to material a person reasonably believed they were entitled to view does not bite. Pressing on past a clear refusal does.
{% /def %}

For sections 2 and 3 there is a further mental element on top: the intent to commit a further offence (s2), or the intent to impair or recklessness as to impairment (s3).

{% takeaway %}
Strong answers tie the knowledge element to a fact in the scenario: a workplace dispute, a guessed password, a warning ignored, a login screen for a different role.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% panel num="06_ORIGIN" eyebrow="WHY THE ACT EXISTS" headline="[R v Gold and Schifreen] 1988." illo="cma-origin" %}
Two journalists used guessed credentials to reach a BT Prestel service and view the Duke of Edinburgh's mailbox. The prosecution tried to fit the conduct under the Forgery and Counterfeiting Act 1981. The House of Lords held that no forgery had taken place because the false credentials existed only momentarily inside the machine. Parliament accepted the gap and passed the Computer Misuse Act 1990 to close it.

{% cloud %}
Amendments: {% chip %}PJA 2006{% /chip %} {% chip %}SCA 2015{% /chip %}
{% /cloud %}

The Police and Justice Act 2006 raised the s1 maximum from six months to two years and added s3A. The Serious Crime Act 2015 added s3ZA for the most serious attacks.

{% takeaway %}
Naming R v Gold is rare in student answers and signals examiner-level depth. The two amendments explain why s3A and s3ZA sit oddly numbered in the modern Act.
{% /takeaway %}
{% /panel %}

{% /panels %}

{% closer line="name the section. [then] apply it." body="Working with a CMA scenario in Paper 1 comes down to two moves, in this order." %}
{% step num="STEP 01" head="Name the section" %}
Identify what the person actually did and pin it to s1, s2, s3, s3ZA or s3A. Use the maximum-sentence ladder to sanity-check the choice.
{% /step %}
{% step num="STEP 02" head="Apply the elements" %}
Quote the scenario back. Show the unauthorised access. Show the knowledge. Show any further intent or recklessness. Name the impact for the worker and the organisation.
{% /step %}
{% /closer %}
